{
  "answer": "# LightSem Audio Understanding: Broad Reassessment\n\nThe prior LightSem results rule out **specific feature recipes**, not source-aware representations, pretrained encoders, transcription, or separation as families. The strongest next direction is a controlled, multi-rate study that preserves stereo, represents sources explicitly, and tests whether information is **absent**, **present but inaccessible**, or **present and ignored downstream**.\n\nNo result from the papers below implies a LightSem gain. Every candidate needs local falsification against Base92/Base94 under artist-family holdouts and intervention tests.\n\n## Current interpretation\n\nThe existing evidence supports these narrower conclusions:\n\n- The old stem-envelope concatenation improved event AP by 0.0185 over 0.1753, narrowly missing a predefined 0.02 gate. That rejects that feature set under that training recipe—not source information.\n- MERT PCA32 added roughly 0.018–0.019 centered AP but exhibited strong song-identity retrieval. That is evidence of potentially useful information mixed with a confound, not universal collapse.\n- MuQ/MERT motion-probe MSE being worse than Base92—and even worse than the mean predictor—shows that their selected layers, pooling, normalization, objective and decoder were poorly matched to the existing motion target.\n- The conditional-energy MuQ score degradation from 0.63418 to 0.63935 rejects that scalar recipe.\n- Rich96 is a visual target and must not be treated as an audio representation.\n- The present Demucs cache has already destroyed stereo evidence by averaging each stem to mono at 22.05 kHz. It cannot answer whether per-source spatial image is useful.\n- Accepted body trajectories remain valid targets. Source reconstruction, body retraining and an autonomously generated motion substitute are not prerequisites for the next study.\n\nThe painful truth: the old tests mostly asked whether a large pretrained representation could be cheaply compressed and appended to a downstream model designed around Base92. They did **not** cleanly ask whether source, rhythmic-interplay or stereo information exists and is usable.\n\n## Literature map\n\n### Separation models\n\n| Work | Representation or output | Temporal granularity | Reproducibility | Main limitation | Falsifiable LightSem test |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **Hybrid Transformers for Music Source Separation**, arXiv:2211.08553 | Stereo waveform estimates for vocals, drums, bass and residual; hybrid waveform/spectrogram model with cross-domain attention | Sample-level outputs; internal multiscale states | [Demucs code](https://github.com/facebookresearch/demucs), pretrained checkpoints | Four broad classes; “other” remains heterogeneous; performance benefited substantially from extra training data [1] | Re-extract 30–50 songs as stereo 44.1 kHz; compare activity/onset/spatial probes against the existing mono cache |\n| **Music Source Separation with Band-Split RoPE Transformer**, arXiv:2309.02612 | Complex-spectrogram masks and separated stereo stems | Sample-level reconstructed audio | Architecture implementations and multiple community checkpoints exist; public models must be tracked individually [2][3] | Checkpoints differ in targets, data and licensing; high separation SDR does not guarantee better semantic events | Run the same instrument-activity and intervention evaluation using a six-stem checkpoint on a small fixed subset |\n| **Mel-Band RoFormer for Music Source Separation**, arXiv:2310.01809 | Mel-informed band splitting with hierarchical temporal/frequency attention | Sample-level output | Public architecture and community weights exist [4][3] | Often optimized for vocals or a specific stem; community recipes are heterogeneous | Test whether guitar/piano slots add information beyond HTDemucs “other,” without changing the downstream model |\n| **A Stem-Agnostic Single-Decoder System for Music Source Separation Beyond Four Stems (Banquet)**, arXiv:2406.18747 | Query-conditioned source waveform; PaSST-derived instrument query plus one band-split decoder | Sample-level output; query is largely class-level | [Official code](https://github.com/kwatcharasupat/query-bandit); evaluated on MoisesDB; 24.9M trainable parameters [5] | Query classification errors and MoisesDB domain dependence; uncommon instruments may be weak | Predefine 8–12 queries relevant to the demos, then test independent source-activity labels and mute sensitivity |\n| **Separate Anything You Describe (AudioSep)**, arXiv:2308.05037 | Text-conditioned open-domain separated waveform | Sample-level output with clip/text conditioning | [Official code and checkpoints](https://github.com/Audio-AGI/AudioSep) are public [6][7] | Text-query ambiguity, leakage and weaker musical specialization than closed-set separators | Query concrete events—“hand percussion,” “female vocal,” “synth bass,” “riser”—and score event precision, not audio plausibility |\n| **SAM Audio: Segment Anything in Audio**, arXiv:2512.18099 | Flow-matching separation conditioned by language, visual masks or temporal spans | Sample-level target source | The paper is valid and covers speech, music and general audio, but code/weight release and licensing must be verified before adoption [8] | Much larger and newer system; compute, release maturity and reproducibility risk | Use only if runnable pretrained weights exist; compare prompted extraction against Banquet/AudioSep on ten manually marked intervals |\n\nHTDemucs remains a valid baseline because its public interface preserves stereo; Demucs was explicitly designed to take a stereo mixture and output stereo source estimates. The current LightSem preprocessing—not Demucs itself—is what discarded the image.[9]\n\nBS-RoFormer is worth testing, but its ecosystem needs legal hygiene. The architecture and training repositories can be MIT while individual checkpoints may be non-commercial or ambiguously licensed; for example, some public vocal models are explicitly CC BY-NC 4.0.[10]\n\n### Source-aware learning\n\n| Work | Objective and output | Granularity | Availability | Known limitation | Falsifiable LightSem test |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **Stem-JEPA: A Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture for Musical Stem Compatibility Estimation**, arXiv:2408.02514 | Predict a held-out compatible stem embedding from the remaining stems | Primarily clip/stem-level compatibility, not a native 40 Hz stream | [Official repository](https://github.com/SonyCSLParis/Stem-JEPA); repository listed as LGPL-3.0 [11][12] | Optimized for compatibility and retrieval; may encode song identity, harmony or genre more strongly than local events | Train only a small LightSem adapter on frozen embeddings; test same-song offset and cross-song same-key/BPM negatives |\n| **Multi-Source Contrastive Learning from Musical Audio**, arXiv:2302.07077 | Associates a song excerpt with an automatically extracted vocal or instrumental source; objective includes source presence/absence | Excerpt-level embedding | [Official code/models](https://github.com/cgaroufis/MSCOL) [13][14] | Pseudo-separator errors become training noise; generic excerpt embeddings may lack event timing | Compare source-activity and instrument-event probes against standard same-excerpt contrastive learning |\n| **COCOLA: Coherence-Oriented Contrastive Learning of Musical Audio Representations**, arXiv:2404.16969 | Bilinear compatibility between disjoint stem submixes; harmonic and percussive variants | Approximately five-second windows in the published recipe | [Official code and checkpoints](https://github.com/gladia-research-group/cocola); models cover MUSDB18-HQ, MoisesDB, Slakh2100 and CocoChorales [15][16] | Designed for accompaniment coherence, not frame-accurate control; compatible clips can share broad song identity | Test discrimination of aligned vs 100/250/500 ms stem offsets while matching song, BPM and loudness |\n| **Leave-One-EquiVariant**, arXiv:2412.18955 | Contrastive subspaces that retain selected augmentation information rather than forcing invariance to everything | Three-second examples in the paper | PyTorch reproduction described by authors [17] | Selected transformations must correspond to desired semantics | Construct separate equivariant heads for time offset, gain, pan and LR swap; test recovery of the applied transform |\n| **PA-HuBERT: Self-Supervised Music Source Separation via Primitive Auditory Clustering and Hidden-Unit BERT**, arXiv:2304.02160 | Clustered auditory units followed by masked-unit prediction and separator fine-tuning | Frame/token sequence | Paper and pipeline description available [18] | More relevant as a pretraining concept than a ready LightSem extractor | Compare its source-sensitive tokens with generic MERT tokens on source mute/swap classification |\n| **StemFX: Learning Mixing Style Representations via Autoregressive FX Chain Prediction on Source-Separated Stems**, arXiv:2607.15634 | Predicts source-specific effects chains from pseudo-stems; uses cross-song stem mixing to suppress content shortcuts | Stem/clip embedding | Paper reports pseudo-stems from roughly 105,000 songs [19] | Very new; optimized for mixing style rather than rhythmic interplay | Test whether its representation predicts known pan/width/effect interventions while failing song-ID retrieval |\n\nThe most relevant idea from this family is not “use JEPA.” It is **construct training pairs whose only reliable solution requires the model to understand source relationships**. Stem swaps, offsets, partial mutes, independent panning and cross-song remixing are therefore more important than the brand name of the objective.\n\n### Foundation encoders\n\n| Work | Pretraining signal | Native output | Availability | Limitation for LightSem | Local test |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **MERT: Acoustic Music Understanding Model with Large-Scale Self-supervised Training**, arXiv:2306.00107 | Music-oriented masked acoustic modeling | MERT-v1 features at 75 Hz and MERT-v0 at 50 Hz | [Official code](https://github.com/yizhilll/MERT) and public checkpoints [20][21] | High-dimensional layers can retain recording and song identity; PCA may remove sparse event directions | Layerwise frozen probes with song-family holdout, whitening fitted only on training songs and no temporal pooling beyond 40 Hz alignment |\n| **MuQ: Self-Supervised Music Representation Learning with Mel Residual Vector Quantization**, arXiv:2501.01108 | Masked prediction of Mel-RVQ tokens with a Conformer | Published base recipe uses a 25 Hz log-mel sequence; 310M parameters | [Official code/checkpoints](https://github.com/tencent-ailab/MuQ) [22][23] | Coarser rate and reconstruction-oriented tokens; a scalar energy head discards almost all representational structure | Probe individual layers and learned 25→40 Hz adapters; compare against shuffled-frame and random-projection controls |\n| **A Foundation Model for Music Informatics (MusicFM)**, arXiv:2311.03318 | BEST-RQ-style masked token prediction with a Conformer | Temporal token sequence | [Official code](https://github.com/minzwon/musicfm) is public [24][25] | Exact extraction cadence and checkpoint behavior are implementation-specific; less source-specific than stem objectives | Run the identical probe protocol used for MERT/MuQ, with no larger decoder or extra context |\n| **BEATs: Audio Pre-Training with Acoustic Tokenizers**, arXiv:2212.09058 | Iterative discrete acoustic tokenizer and masked-token prediction | About 50 Hz frame features | [Microsoft implementation and checkpoints](https://github.com/microsoft/unilm/tree/master/beats) [26][27][28] | General AudioSet semantics may help transient/event detection but underrepresent music structure | Probe percussion, vocal activity and non-musical FX events; compare with MERT under matched width |\n| **Large-Scale Contrastive Language-Audio Pretraining (CLAP)**, arXiv:2211.06687 | Audio–text contrastive learning | Primarily clip-level semantic embedding; internal token maps are model-dependent | [Official code and checkpoints](https://github.com/LAION-AI/CLAP), trained with public audio-caption sources [29][30] | Global text semantics are too coarse for motion timing unless intermediate maps are exposed | Use as query/tag supervision only; reject if it cannot localize manually annotated source events |\n| **Masked Autoencoders that Listen (Audio-MAE)**, arXiv:2207.06405 | Spectrogram-patch reconstruction | Patch-token sequence | [Official code/models](https://github.com/facebookresearch/AudioMAE) [31][32] | Patch size and AudioSet training encourage coarser events; not music-specialized | Test instrument-activity and transient probes at matched effective frame rate |\n| **Audio-JEPA**, arXiv:2507.02915 | Predicts masked audio embeddings rather than reconstructing samples | Spectrogram-token sequence | Code/checkpoint claims require release verification; the paper distinguishes it from stem-masked Stem-JEPA [33] | General audio patch prediction does not inherently discover instruments or source interaction | Compare generic patch masking with whole-source masking on the same multitrack subset |\n\nMERT, MuQ and MusicFM remain legitimate candidates. The correction is methodological: never judge a pretrained encoder by one PCA32 or scalar-conditioning recipe, and never let it receive a larger downstream head than Base92 unless decoder capacity is independently controlled.\n\n### Rhythm and structure\n\n| Work | Supervision and output | Resolution | Reproducibility | Limitation | Local test |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **Beat This! Accurate Beat Tracking without DBN Postprocessing**, arXiv:2407.21658 | Supervised beat/downbeat frame probabilities and event times | Native frame stream around 50 Hz | [Official MIT code, models and annotation repository](https://github.com/CPJKU/beat_this) [34][35][36] | Difficult and underrepresented genres remain failure cases; continuity metrics can be worse despite event F1 [34] | Preserve native probabilities, entropy and local peak geometry; test syncopated/per-source onset labels rather than only global beats |\n| **All-In-One Metrical and Functional Structure Analysis with Neighborhood Attentions on Demixed Audio**, arXiv:2307.16425 | Joint tempo, beat, downbeat, segment boundary and functional-label prediction from demixed spectrograms | Frame activations plus sparse events and segment intervals | [Official MIT repository](https://github.com/mir-aidj/all-in-one) and public weights [37][38][39] | Demixing dependency and Harmonix-style domain bias; functional labels are coarse | Compare only its additional segment/boundary tokens against Base94, leaving Beat This inputs unchanged |\n| **MT3: Multi-Task Multitrack Music Transcription**, arXiv:2111.03017 | Sequence-to-sequence MIDI-like note, timing, program and instrument tokens | Event-token timing rather than fixed-rate motion frames | [Official repository](https://github.com/magenta/mt3); inference and pretrained checkpoint are available, but the official release has been criticized for lacking the full training path [40][41] | Instrument leakage, quantization and polyphonic transcription errors | Convert tokens into 40 Hz onset/sustain/release maps; score against independent note/drum labels before using them downstream |\n| **YourMT3+: Multi-instrument Music Transcription with Enhanced Transformer Architectures and Cross-dataset Stem Augmentation**, arXiv:2407.04822 | MIDI-like multi-instrument and vocal tokens using enhanced T5/PerceiverTF encoders | Operates on 2.048-second windows; YMT3 front end has 256 time steps, while outputs remain event tokens | [Official code](https://github.com/mimbres/YourMT3) and [checkpoints](https://huggingface.co/mimbres/YourMT3) [42][43] | Token decoding is expensive and can hallucinate or misassign instruments | Compare source-specific onset maps against Banquet/HTDemucs energy-onset maps on the same songs |\n| **MR-MT3: Memory Retaining Multi-Track Music Transcription**, arXiv:2403.10024 | Adds memory retention, prior-token sampling and token shuffling to reduce instrument leakage | Event-token sequence | Research implementation; evaluated on Slakh2100 [40][44] | Synthetic-domain dependence and remaining assignment errors | Measure whether repeated phrases preserve instrument identity better than MT3 |\n| **ADTOF: A Large Dataset of Non-Synthetic Music for Automatic Drum Transcription**, arXiv:2111.11737 | Supervised drum-onset classes from crowdsourced rhythm-game annotations | Sparse drum events; common models use roughly 10 ms analysis hops | Code/models and a 359-hour mel-spectrogram dataset are available by request under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 [45][46] | Crowdsourced label noise, no universally redistributable raw audio and drum-centric vocabulary | Train or reuse a drum head, then evaluate kick/snare/hi-hat AP on MDB-Drums or manually marked LightSem excerpts |\n\nFor hard rhythms, the missing object is probably not another global beat probability. More promising targets are:\n\n- Source-specific onset probabilities.\n- Kick–snare–hat or low/mid/high percussion events.\n- Beat phase and bar phase.\n- On-beat versus off-beat source activity.\n- Pairwise source offsets and call/response.\n- Syncopation proxies.\n- Phrase boundaries and section roles.\n\nBeat This and All-In-One are complementary: Beat This offers strong local metrical activations, while All-In-One introduces slower functional structure and explicitly demonstrates a model operating over demixed audio with joint metrical/structural supervision.[38]\n\n## Stereo information\n\n### What is identifiable\n\nA mastered stereo file supports estimation of its **two-channel image**, including:\n\n- Left/right energy balance.\n- Mid/side energy and width.\n- Frequency-dependent inter-channel level difference.\n- Frequency-dependent inter-channel phase difference.\n- Magnitude-squared coherence.\n- Correlated versus decorrelated ambience.\n- Source-relative pan and width after separation.\n- Changes under LR swap, synthetic pan and width transformations.\n\nIt generally does **not** identify physical 3D direction of arrival. Commercial masters may combine close microphones, artificial reverberation, delay, stereo widening, automated panning, doubled performances and sampled instruments, without known microphone geometry. LightSem should therefore name these features `pan`, `width`, `coherence` or `master-space image`, not `azimuth` or physical DOA.\n\n### Candidate features\n\nFor each mixture and source slot, preserve stereo at 44.1 kHz and derive:\n\n$$\nM=\\frac{L+R}{2},\\qquad S=\\frac{L-R}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{pan}=\\frac{E_R-E_L}{E_R+E_L+\\epsilon}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{ILD}(f,t)=20\\log_{10}\\frac{|X_L(f,t)|+\\epsilon}{|X_R(f,t)|+\\epsilon}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{IPD}(f,t)=\\angle\\left(X_L(f,t)X_R(f,t)^*\\right)\n$$\n\nThe model should receive bandwise $$\\sin(\\mathrm{IPD})$$ and $$\\cos(\\mathrm{IPD})$$, not raw wrapped phase. Width can be represented by side-to-mid energy, while coherence should use normalized cross-spectral statistics over a short rolling window.\n\nA practical 40 Hz stereo bank might contain:\n\n- Mixture pan, width and coherence in 4–8 frequency bands.\n- The same features per separated source.\n- Per-source activity confidence to suppress meaningless spatial estimates during silence.\n- Relative source pan and relative width.\n- Pan velocity and width velocity.\n- Correlated transient versus diffuse-tail energy.\n\n### Counterfactual tests\n\n- **LR swap:** signed pan and IPD features should flip appropriately; mono activity, beat and width magnitude should remain stable.\n- **Equal-power pan:** source pan features should track the known injected value monotonically.\n- **Width scaling:** side-related features should respond while source activity remains stable.\n- **Mono fold-down:** width and signed pan should collapse; semantic source features should remain usable.\n- **Source swap:** slot identity should change while global rhythm remains mostly invariant.\n- **Source offset:** interaction features should detect misalignment even when source identity and loudness are unchanged.\n\nFailure of these basic tests means the representation should not reach the LightSem model.\n\n## Dataset reality\n\n| Dataset | Useful supervision | Access and license | Domain gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **MUSDB18-HQ** | 150 songs with stereo mixture and vocals/drums/bass/other stems | Full audio is access-controlled through Zenodo and restricted to academic use; the MIT license of the `musdb` parser is not the audio license [47][48][49] | Small, mostly conventional production and only four broad stems |\n| **Slakh2100** | 2,100 synthetic multitracks, MIDI, program labels and known note timing; 145 hours | Dataset CC BY 4.0; generation code MIT [50][51] | Clean symbolic alignment but synthetic instruments, programmed timing and limited real mixing artifacts |\n| **MoisesDB** | 240 professionally produced songs with up to 11 instrument categories | Request-based access; downstream projects commonly retain non-commercial/share-alike restrictions, so the official terms must be reviewed rather than inferred from code repositories [52][53] | Better class breadth, but small and potentially different from psychedelic/electropop production |\n| **MedleyDB** | Real multitracks with detailed instrument activation; MDB-Drums adds fine drum events | Audio availability and Creative Commons terms vary by track; preserve per-track attribution and restrictions | Real multitrack recording, but many tracks are less densely produced than commercial masters |\n| **ENST-Drums** | Three drummers, several kits, solo and accompanied recordings; 20 percussion classes | Research download; verify audio terms before redistribution | Strong isolated-drum detail, weak coverage of electronic and heavily processed percussion |\n| **MDB-Drums** | 23 fully produced MedleyDB tracks with detailed drum-onset classes [54] | Inherits MedleyDB track-level terms | Real but very small; useful primarily as independent evaluation |\n| **ADTOF** | Approximately 359 hours of non-synthetic drum annotations distributed as mel spectrograms | Available by request; repository material is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 [45] | Large but crowdsourced, drum-centric and detached from freely usable raw masters |\n| **Harmonix Set** | Beat, downbeat and functional section structure used by All-In-One | Annotation/audio access terms must be handled separately | Popular-music structure labels help phrase modeling but do not provide source-level ground truth |\n\nSlakh is the cleanest place to ask whether a representation can encode note-level source interplay. MUSDB18-HQ, MoisesDB and MedleyDB are better for real mixing and source activity. None alone matches Shpongle or AURORA closely enough to justify a single-dataset conclusion.\n\n## Proposed representation\n\nExplicit source slots plus multi-rate rhythm and phrase tokens are a strong candidate, but they should remain one hypothesis among several.\n\n### Fast stream: 40 Hz\n\nMaintain compatibility with Base92/Base94:\n\n- Existing Base92.\n- Beat This beat/downbeat probabilities.\n- Per-source activity, RMS, flux, onset and transientness.\n- Per-source tonal/noise ratio and broad pitch/chroma evidence.\n- Stereo pan, width, ILD/IPD and coherence.\n- Source-separator confidence or residual ratio.\n- Optional frozen-encoder projection at native or near-native rate.\n\nDo not pool source features into one concatenated envelope before evaluation. Preserve source identity as a slot dimension and let a small shared slot encoder operate across sources.\n\n### Interaction stream: 10 Hz\n\nRepresent relationships rather than another copy of energy:\n\n- Pairwise onset coincidence.\n- Relative onset lag.\n- Beat-phase-conditioned source activity.\n- Drum–bass lock.\n- Vocal–accompaniment call/response.\n- Harmonic compatibility.\n- Source density and orchestration changes.\n- Stereo-image divergence between sources.\n\nThis can align naturally with the existing 10 Hz accent/chromatic/palette processing without forcing every feature through four-frame average pooling.\n\n### Phrase stream: events or 1–2 Hz\n\nUse:\n\n- Downbeat/bar index.\n- Tempo and meter confidence.\n- Section-boundary probability.\n- Functional segment embedding.\n- Repetition/novelty.\n- Phrase-level source entrance and exit.\n- Long-range intensity trajectory.\n\nAll-In-One is the obvious initial pseudo-labeler, but its labels remain teacher outputs—not ground truth.\n\n## Missing versus ignored\n\nThis is the central design problem. A downstream no-gain result is ambiguous unless the study separates six stages.\n\n| Observation | Likely diagnosis | Required follow-up |\n|---|---|---|\n| Teacher output disagrees with independent labels | Missing or corrupted information at extraction | Change separator/teacher or reject that pseudo-label |\n| Teacher agrees, but a frozen linear/nonlinear probe fails | Adapter, temporal alignment or compression destroyed information | Probe earlier layers, native cadence and higher-dimensional projections |\n| Probe succeeds, but LightSem does not improve | Downstream ignores the feature or the current target does not reward it | Measure gradients, attention, ablations and target-conditional effects |\n| Counterfactual changes the representation but not LightSem output | Downstream inactivity | Feature-only training, stronger gating or auxiliary loss |\n| Counterfactual changes LightSem output in the wrong direction | Spurious shortcut or poor equivariance | Add intervention supervision; remove song/artist leakage |\n| Oracle label improves LightSem but pseudo-label does not | Teacher quality is limiting | Improve teacher or calibrate confidence |\n| Neither oracle nor pseudo-label improves LightSem | Information is probably irrelevant to the present target/model scale | Stop that branch unless aesthetic evaluation contradicts it |\n| Encoder works on random song split but fails artist-family holdout | Song/production identity leakage | Reject the result |\n| Feature variance is high but effective rank is low | Representation is active but collapsed to a few nuisance dimensions | Whitening, layer selection or structured slots |\n| Native representation is rich but projected feature is inactive | Projection bottleneck | Increase width or use supervised/task-aware projection |\n\n### Required diagnostic ladder\n\n1. **Teacher validity:** compare extracted source activity, beats, drum events and pan interventions against independent labels.\n2. **Information probe:** freeze representation and train matched-capacity probes.\n3. **Projection audit:** compare native, PCA, random projection, supervised projection and learned bottleneck.\n4. **Utilization audit:** inspect permutation sensitivity, gradients, gating magnitude and output change.\n5. **Target relevance:** test existing 139 native motion channels and independently labeled event targets.\n6. **Rendered relevance:** evaluate emitted DMX.\n7. **Aesthetic relevance:** conduct blinded, within-song paired comparisons.\n\nA probe should never receive more contextual capacity than the baseline unless a capacity-matched Base92 control receives the same decoder.\n\n## Evaluation protocol\n\n### Splits\n\n- Hold out complete artists, song families, remixes and production lineages.\n- Prevent segments from the same song entering multiple splits.\n- Create a similar-BPM evaluation subset so tempo cannot solve source/rhythm tasks.\n- Report the two demo songs separately; do not combine Shpongle and AURORA into one pseudo-generalization claim.\n- Fit normalization, PCA, whitening and codebooks only on training artists.\n\n### Independent targets\n\nUse separate evaluation labels for:\n\n- Source presence and instrument family.\n- Source entrances and exits.\n- Drum onsets by class.\n- Beat and downbeat.\n- Source-relative onset offset.\n- Section boundaries.\n- Known synthetic pan, width, mute and LR transforms.\n- Existing native motion channels.\n- Final emitted DMX behavior.\n\nTeacher–teacher agreement is useful for debugging but is not ground truth.\n\n### Mandatory controls\n\nEvery probe or LightSem study should include:\n\n- Raw waveform or raw log-mel where feasible.\n- Random features matched in dimension and variance.\n- Temporally shuffled features.\n- Across-song shuffled features.\n- Per-song mean.\n- Global mean.\n- Base92.\n- Base94.\n- Existing mono stem envelopes.\n- Stereo source features.\n- MERT under the corrected protocol.\n- Randomly initialized encoder with the same architecture.\n- Label-permuted training.\n- Feature-only and baseline-plus-feature models.\n- Capacity-matched wider Base92/Base94 models.\n\nThe same-BPM and within-song offset controls are especially important. Otherwise a model can appear to understand rhythmic interplay while only recovering tempo or song identity.\n\n### Representation diagnostics\n\nReport separately:\n\n- Per-channel variance.\n- Effective covariance rank.\n- Temporal autocorrelation.\n- Cross-song versus within-song distances.\n- Artist/song retrieval.\n- Source retrieval.\n- Native-to-projected information loss.\n- Fraction of inactive projected channels.\n- Gradient norm entering each feature family.\n- Output sensitivity to feature permutation.\n- Output sensitivity to source interventions.\n\n“High variance” does not prove use, and “low downstream weight” does not prove no information.\n\n### Metrics\n\nUse task-specific metrics:\n\n- Event AP and F1 with multiple timing tolerances.\n- Source-activity mAP.\n- Onset F1 by instrument.\n- Beat/downbeat F1 and continuity metrics.\n- Section-boundary F1 at fine and coarse tolerances.\n- Pan/width intervention error.\n- Source-offset ordering accuracy.\n- Motion-channel MSE and correlation.\n- Per-channel movement inactivity.\n- DMX fixture-group activation and transition statistics.\n- Blinded pairwise aesthetic preference.\n\nDo not merge probe accuracy, native-motion prediction, emitted-DMX behavior and aesthetic preference into one score.\n\n## Ranked experiments\n\n### 1. Stereo-preserving source audit\n\n**Why first:** it repairs an information-destroying preprocessing choice without requiring body reconstruction or model retraining.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Re-extract a stratified subset with HTDemucs while retaining stereo and 44.1 kHz.\n- Keep waveform outputs and derive source activity, onset and spatial features.\n- Compare against the current mono 22.05 kHz cache.\n- Run LR swap, pan, mono-fold and source-mute tests.\n- Do not train LightSem yet.\n\n**Falsification:** reject stereo source features if they cannot recover known interventions or improve independently labeled source/event probes over mixture stereo and mono stems.\n\n### 2. Source slots at 40 Hz\n\n**Why second:** the old stem trial collapsed source information into envelopes before establishing whether source identity or interaction mattered.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Four initial slots: drums, bass, vocals, residual.\n- Shared small slot encoder.\n- Activity/confidence mask.\n- Keep slots explicit until after cross-source attention.\n- Include Base94 as a separate stream.\n- Compare explicit slots with flat concatenation and source-averaged pooling.\n\n**Falsification:** reject the slot design if flat or shuffled-slot controls perform identically under source mute/swap tests.\n\n### 3. Multi-rate rhythm tokens\n\n**Why third:** LightSem already has a strong global beat teacher, so the next marginal information is source-specific rhythm and phrase structure.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- 40 Hz Beat This probabilities and source onsets.\n- 10 Hz pairwise source-interaction features.\n- Event/slow All-In-One section tokens.\n- Drum transcription pseudo-labels where confidence is high.\n- Evaluate hard-rhythm excerpts separately.\n\n**Falsification:** reject the extra streams if they do not beat Base94 on independently annotated off-beat, syncopation, source-offset and entrance events.\n\n### 4. Oracle-versus-teacher experiment\n\n**Why fourth:** this is the cheapest clean separation between missing information and a poor teacher.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- On multitrack datasets, compute features once from true stems and once from separated pseudo-stems.\n- Train identical probes and LightSem-style adapters.\n- Include mixture-only and shuffled-source controls.\n\n**Interpretation:**\n\n- True stems help, pseudo-stems do not: separator quality is limiting.\n- Both help: source representation is valid.\n- Neither helps but source probes succeed: downstream targets ignore source information.\n- Neither source probes nor targets improve: stop that specific representation.\n\n### 5. Source-aware contrastive adapter\n\n**Why fifth:** MSCOL, Stem-JEPA and COCOLA provide useful objective patterns, but training an entire foundation encoder would be premature.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Freeze Base92/Base94 plus source feature extractors.\n- Train a small adapter with aligned source pairs.\n- Use within-song temporal offsets, source swaps and cross-song same-BPM negatives.\n- Add explicit transformation heads for mute, offset and pan.\n- Prevent artist identity from being a valid positive cue.\n\n**Falsification:** reject if gains vanish under artist-family holdout or if song retrieval rises without source/event improvement.\n\n### 6. Transcription-derived event maps\n\n**Why sixth:** symbolic events provide interpretable instrument timing, but transcription errors can be worse than separator errors.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Compare MT3/YourMT3+ tokens with simpler drum/onset heads.\n- Convert tokens to instrument-specific onset/sustain/release maps.\n- Preserve confidence and missing-event masks.\n- Never feed raw token IDs directly into the motion model.\n\n**Falsification:** reject a transcription teacher whose event maps are worse than stem spectral-flux baselines on independent labels.\n\n### 7. Corrected foundation-encoder benchmark\n\n**Why seventh:** MERT/MuQ/MusicFM are broad but expensive and vulnerable to identity leakage.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Test layers separately.\n- Preserve native time resolution.\n- Use train-only whitening.\n- Compare PCA, random projection and supervised bottlenecks.\n- Match downstream width and parameter count.\n- Include MERT, MuQ, MusicFM and BEATs.\n- Use CLAP only as semantic/tag supervision.\n\n**Falsification:** reject an encoder only if native and appropriately adapted features fail matched probes and intervention tests—not because one low-dimensional concat fails LightSem.\n\n### 8. Separator diversity study\n\n**Why eighth:** newer separators are potentially valuable, but better audio separation is not automatically better representation learning.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Compare stereo HTDemucs, one six-stem BS-RoFormer and Banquet.\n- Add AudioSep for selected unusual-source queries.\n- Consider SAM Audio only after confirming runnable weights and licensing.\n- Evaluate source labels, leakage, residual energy, intervention consistency and compute.\n\n**Falsification:** do not adopt a newer separator if its extra stems lack label reliability or add no information beyond subdividing noise.\n\n### 9. Stem-level JEPA pretraining\n\n**Why ninth:** promising but more expensive, and its natural output is clip compatibility rather than dense motion features.\n\n**Recipe:**\n\n- Start on Slakh2100 plus a smaller real multitrack corpus.\n- Mask entire sources, time spans or source-time blocks.\n- Predict separate fast-event and slow-compatibility embeddings.\n- Use cross-song remixing to break song identity.\n- Compare JEPA, contrastive and supervised source-event objectives.\n\n**Falsification:** stop if it only improves stem compatibility while failing event localization and LightSem utilization tests.\n\n## Suggested minimal study\n\nNo new GPU experiments are required now. The study can be prepared as a fixed protocol and executed later in four stages.\n\n### Stage A: cache correction\n\n- Preserve original master stereo.\n- Re-extract stereo HTDemucs stems for a representative subset.\n- Store features at their native rates plus timestamp metadata.\n- Do not resample everything permanently to 10 or 40 Hz.\n- Record separator model, checkpoint hash, code license and weight terms.\n\n### Stage B: label pack\n\nCreate a small, independently annotated benchmark containing:\n\n- Source entrances/exits.\n- Kick/snare/hat and salient percussion.\n- Beat/downbeat corrections.\n- Hard rhythmic interplay.\n- Obvious pan/width changes.\n- Section boundaries.\n- Fixture-relevant moments in each demo.\n\nEven 20–30 carefully labeled excerpts would be more diagnostic than another whole-corpus teacher-agreement score.\n\n### Stage C: probe matrix\n\nRun frozen probes in this order:\n\n1. Base92.\n2. Base94.\n3. Old stem envelopes.\n4. Stereo mixture spatial bank.\n5. Stereo source slots.\n6. Rhythm/structure tokens.\n7. MERT corrected.\n8. MuQ corrected.\n9. MusicFM or BEATs.\n10. Combined source plus rhythm model.\n\nStop candidates that fail independent labels, intervention consistency or holdout generalization.\n\n### Stage D: LightSem integration\n\nOnly surviving representations enter:\n\n- The existing 139-channel native-body prediction task.\n- Source mute/swap/offset tests.\n- Emitted-DMX analysis.\n- Blinded aesthetic comparisons.\n\nPreserve the accepted P2 color policy, generated palette history, 300 ms transitions and two-group behavior. The study should ask whether the new audio representation makes existing trajectories and emitted control more responsive—not silently replace the visual policy or invent a new motion target.\n\n## Decision gates\n\nA representation is worth continuing only if it passes all applicable gates:\n\n- Beats a dimension-matched random feature baseline.\n- Beats temporally and across-song shuffled versions.\n- Survives artist/song-family holdout.\n- Survives a similar-BPM subset.\n- Responds correctly to the intervention it claims to encode.\n- Retains useful native rank and temporal variance.\n- Shows downstream utilization rather than merely probe accessibility.\n- Produces a measurable improvement in the intended target.\n- Does not rely solely on teacher agreement.\n- Separately improves emitted DMX or blinded aesthetics if that is the final claim.\n\nThe likely winner is not necessarily one universal encoder. A smaller combination—Base94, stereo-aware source slots, source-specific onset maps and slow phrase tokens—has a better inductive match to LightSem than another giant pooled song embedding. But that remains a hypothesis, not a preordained architecture.",
